<div class="page">
  <header class="head">
    <div>
      <h1>Chaitanya Patwardhan</h1>
      <p class="title"><b>Operations Manager</b> · Baltimore, Maryland</p>
    </div>
    <div class="contact">
      <a href="mailto:chaitanya.patwardhan40@gmail.com">chaitanya.patwardhan40@gmail.com</a>
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/chaitanya-patwardhan/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">linkedin.com/in/chaitanya-patwardhan</a>
      <a class="cta" href="Chaitanya_Patwardhan_Resume.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Résumé (PDF)</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <p class="summary">I run manufacturing, supply chain and shipping for a company that designs and builds automated parcel-sorting systems, leading a team of 15. My pattern is consistent across every role: walk into an undefined or person-dependent process, fix it, and standardize it so it stays fixed. When the right tool doesn&#x27;t exist, I build it, increasingly with AI, and put it to work on the floor.</p>

  <dl class="facts"><div><dt>Experience</dt><dd>6 years in manufacturing operations and quality</dd></div><div><dt>Education</dt><dd>M.S. Manufacturing &amp; Mechanical Systems Integration, RIT · B.Tech Mechanical Engineering</dd></div><div><dt>Methods</dt><dd>Lean Six Sigma (DMAIC / DMADV), VSM, PFMEA, SPC, PPAP, ERP implementation</dd></div><div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>AI-assisted automation (Claude) for shop-floor tasks, Python, Sage 100 ERP, Power BI, Minitab, JMP, Excel</dd></div></dl>

  <ul class="results"><li><b>89 → 95%</b><span>on-time delivery as Operations Manager</span></li><li><b>−12%</b><span>operating cost, Lean programme</span></li><li><b>−43% / −54%</b><span>module and kit build time, DMAIC capstone</span></li><li><b>3 weeks → 2 days</b><span>to program 500 sensors, a 90% time saving, with a script I wrote</span></li></ul>

  <main class="work">
    <h2 class="sel">Projects</h2>
    
    <section class="employer">
      
      <article class="project has-media">
        <div class="text">
          <div class="ph"><h3>PPAP Manager</h3><span class="when">2026</span></div>
          <p class="outcome">A quality-submission platform that replaces the email thread and the 14-tab spreadsheet.</p>
          <p class="body">Manages all 18 PPAP elements across supplier and customer networks. Suppliers build the package; it locks on submit; customers comment or sign. Cpk/Ppk, Gage R&amp;R and FMEA RPN compute as measurements are typed.</p>
          <ul class="points"><li>One-click PSW and full-package PDF export, plus CSV</li><li>Runs entirely in the browser: no account, no install, and data stays on the user&#x27;s machine</li></ul>
          <p class="tools"><span>Tools</span>Web application, deployed on Vercel<a class="live" href="https://ppap-manager.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View live<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span></a></p>
        </div>
        <figure class="media browser"><div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="PPAP Manager"></figure>
      </article>
      <article class="project has-media">
        <div class="text">
          <div class="ph"><h3>Outbound Shipment Tracker</h3><span class="when">2026</span></div>
          <p class="outcome">One board for every outbound pallet, so the whole company stops asking the shipping team.</p>
          <p class="body">The shipping team logs each shipment as it leaves the dock: carrier, tracking, packing slip, photos, freight cost. Everyone else looks up a kit or part number and answers their own question: when did it ship, where is it, is it in stock.</p>
          <ul class="points"><li>Multi-carrier tracking (Estes, UPS, FedEx, USPS, DHL), kit lookup, inventory locator, per-program dashboards</li><li>Password-gated with encrypted records; works on any phone; migration to Azure and Microsoft Lists planned</li></ul>
          <p class="tools"><span>Tools</span>Web application · Sage 100 exports · GitHub · Vercel</p>
          <p class="note">Not linked here because it holds company data. If you would like to see it, get in touch and I will walk you through a demo.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="media browser"><div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Outbound Shipment Tracker"></figure>
      </article>
      <article class="project has-media">
        <div class="text">
          <div class="ph"><h3>Banner K50Z sensor auto-programmer</h3><span class="when">2026</span></div>
          <p class="outcome">500 sensors programmed in 2 days instead of 3 weeks, a 90% reduction in programming time.</p>
          <p class="body">A Python script I wrote with Claude Cowork that drives Banner&#x27;s configuration software directly: it detects the COM port, connects, loads the approved configuration file, writes it, disconnects, and announces the result out loud so a technician can work through a bin of sensors without looking up. I specified the workflow and the checks, tested it on the bench, and iterated the code with the AI until it ran unattended.</p>
          <ul class="points"><li>About 6 minutes per sensor instead of about an hour (90% less), 19 working days saved per rollout</li><li>Identical, approved configuration on every unit, with no manual re-entry</li></ul>
          <p class="tools"><span>Tools</span>Python · pywinauto · pyserial · Windows UI Automation · Claude Cowork</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="media"><video src="data:video/mp4;base64," poster="data:image/jpeg;base64," controls muted playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9" aria-label="Demo of Banner K50Z sensor auto-programmer"></video></figure>
      </article>
      <article class="project has-media">
        <div class="text">
          <div class="ph"><h3>Inventory accuracy at customer sites</h3><span class="when">Jan – Mar 2025</span></div>
          <p class="outcome">Cycle-count accuracy from 90.7% to 97.9% at one customer site and from 93.7% to 99.5% at another, in three months.</p>
          <p class="body">We hold spare-parts inventory at the customer sites where our sorters are installed, and weekly cycle counts at two of them were failing. Tracing the discrepancies pointed to three causes: parts signed out on work orders with the wrong part number or no work order at all, parts taken mid-count, and look-alike belts and rollers stored and mislabelled together. The fixes were procedural: work-order completion made mandatory with a sign-out sheet as the fallback, and the parts cage reorganized so like parts sit together.</p>
          <ul class="points"><li>About 6,600 parts counted across 24 weekly counts; the second site reached 100% in the final week</li><li>The case study behind the DMAIC-R paper below; the recurring count is the Revisit stage in practice</li></ul>
          <p class="tools"><span>Tools</span>Lean Six Sigma · DMAIC · cycle counting · root cause analysis · Excel</p>
        </div>
